   "subject": "Estimation of item interceps with MLR",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Given a simple non-sense model<div><br></div><div><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(176,22,0)\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0)\">HS.model</span><span style=\"color:rgb(7,26,153)\"> &lt;- </span>&#39; test  =~ x1 + x2 + x3 + x4 + x5&#39; </p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(176,22,0)\"><br></p></div><div>there are 10 free parameters to estimate: 4 factor loadings, 1 factor variance, 5 indicator variances. Since we have p = 5 indicators there are p(p+1)/2 = 15 information elements in the variance-covariance-matrix. The model is overidentified, df = 15-10 = 5. </div><div>When I fit the model with</div><div><br></div><div><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><a href=\"http://fit.ml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://fit.ml&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792432609582000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2U08visWU9XJykKFJu_aB6\">fit.ml</a><span style=\"color:#071a99\"> &lt;- cfa(</span>HS.model<span style=\"color:#071a99\">, </span>data<span style=\"color:#071a99\"> = </span>HolzingerSwineford1939<span style=\"color:#071a99\">)</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#071a99\">summary(</span><a href=\"http://fit.ml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://fit.ml&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792432609582000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2U08visWU9XJykKFJu_aB6\">fit.ml</a><span style=\"color:#071a99\">,</span><span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span>fit.measures<span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span><span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span><span style=\"color:#c38809\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">)</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153)\">parTable(<span style=\"color:#000000\"><a href=\"http://fit.ml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://fit.ml&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792432609582000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2U08visWU9XJykKFJu_aB6\">fit.ml</a></span>)</p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><br></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"><br></p><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153)\"></p><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px;text-indent:0px\">the output shows exactly what I mentioned above: df = 5, free parameters = 10. </div><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px;text-indent:0px\"><br></div><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px;text-indent:0px\">When I fit the model using MLR estimator with </div><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px;text-indent:0px\"><br></div><div style=\"color:rgb(34,34,34);font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;font-size:13px;text-indent:0px\"><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\">fit.mlr<span style=\"color:#071a99\"> &lt;- cfa(</span>HS.model<span style=\"color:#071a99\">, </span>data<span style=\"color:#071a99\"> = </span>HolzingerSwineford1939<span style=\"color:#071a99\">, </span><span style=\"color:#c38809\"> </span>estimator<span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span><span style=\"color:#b01600\">&quot;MLR&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">)</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#071a99\">summary(</span>fit.mlr<span style=\"color:#071a99\">,</span><span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span>fit.measures<span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span><span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span><span style=\"color:#c38809\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">)</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153)\">parTable(<span style=\"color:#000000\">fit.mlr</span>)</p></div><div><br></div><p></p></div><div> the intercepts are also estimated which means an increase of free parameters of 5, so 15 all together. But the summary still shows df = 5. <br></div><div><br></div><div>So my questions are: </div><div><br></div><div>1) Why does the MLR estimator automatically estimate the item intercepts?</div><div>2) Why does this increase in free parameters not lead to a decrease in degrees of freedom?</div><div><br></div><div>This also happens in more complex models.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks a lot, </div><div><br></div><div>Tobias</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>-----</div><div><br></div><div>The code to reproduce my issue:</div><div><br></div><div><p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153)\">library(<span style=\"color:#000000\">lavaan</span>)</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153);min-height:15px\"><br></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(176,22,0)\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">HS.model</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\"> &lt;- </span>&#39; visual  =~ x1 + x2 + x3 +x4 + x5&#39;</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153);min-height:15px\"><br></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><a href=\"http://fit.ml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://fit.ml&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792432609582000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2U08visWU9XJykKFJu_aB6\">fit.ml</a><span style=\"color:#071a99\"> &lt;- cfa(</span>HS.model<span style=\"color:#071a99\">, </span>data<span style=\"color:#071a99\"> = </span>HolzingerSwineford1939<span style=\"color:#071a99\">)</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#071a99\">summary(</span><a href=\"http://fit.ml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://fit.ml&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792432609582000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2U08visWU9XJykKFJu_aB6\">fit.ml</a><span style=\"color:#071a99\">,</span><span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span>fit.measures<span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span><span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span><span style=\"color:#c38809\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">)</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153)\">parTable(<span style=\"color:#000000\"><a href=\"http://fit.ml\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://fit.ml&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792432609582000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2U08visWU9XJykKFJu_aB6\">fit.ml</a></span>)</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153);min-height:15px\"><br></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153);min-height:15px\"><br></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\">fit.mlr<span style=\"color:#071a99\"> &lt;- cfa(</span>HS.model<span style=\"color:#071a99\">, </span>data<span style=\"color:#071a99\"> = </span>HolzingerSwineford1939<span style=\"color:#071a99\">, </span><span style=\"color:#c38809\"> </span>estimator<span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span><span style=\"color:#b01600\">&quot;MLR&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">)</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco\"><span style=\"color:#071a99\">summary(</span>fit.mlr<span style=\"color:#071a99\">,</span><span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span>fit.measures<span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">=</span><span style=\"color:#b01600\"> </span><span style=\"color:#c38809\">TRUE</span><span style=\"color:#071a99\">)</span></p>\r\n<p style=\"margin-bottom:0px;margin-left:4px;font-size:11px;line-height:normal;font-family:Monaco;color:rgb(7,26,153)\">parTable(<span style=\"color:#000000\">fit.mlr</span>)</p></div></div>"
    ]
